### [@mastra/e2b@0.14.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/e2b@0.14.0/workspaces/e2b/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Added `E2BFactorySandbox`, which plugs E2B into Mastra Factory as the sandbox provider. Factory gets one sandbox per session, a repository template it can build ahead of the first session, and user-tunable settings. (#26467)

  ```ts
  import { MastraFactory } from '@mastra/factory';
  import { E2BFactorySandbox } from '@mastra/e2b';

  new MastraFactory({
    sandbox: new E2BFactorySandbox(), // reads E2B_API_KEY
  });
  ```

  **Settings:** `cpuCount`, `memoryMb` and `idleTimeoutMinutes`. Unset values fall back to the `defaults` option, then to 2 CPUs, 1024 MB and 5 minutes.

  **Builds:** starts a template build in the background, reports its status with logs, and lists the template's build history.

### [@mastra/factory@0.22.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/factory@0.22.0/mastracode/factory/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Added environment builds. When the Factory sandbox implements the `builds` capability, Factory builds the environment template ahead of sessions instead of on the first session boot. (#26479)

  - `POST /web/factory/projects/:id/environment/build` starts a build now (the Environment page's **Build now** button, or `mastra api factory project environment build <project-id>`).
  - `GET /web/factory/projects/:id/environment/builds` and `GET /web/factory/projects/:id/environment/builds/:buildId` read the provider's build history and live status.
  - The environment payload carries `buildTriggers`: a cron schedule and a push trigger with a debounce window.
  - Scheduled and push builds skip when the last build is ready and nothing it was built from changed. Only one build starts at a time, and no trigger starts a build while the last one is still running. Saving settings never starts a build.
  - Hosts that spread `prepare()`'s result into `new Mastra` pick up the `factory-environment-build` workflow.
  - After a session's own setup, each repository link records `configured` or `failed` with the redacted error.

  Turn on a nightly rebuild and push builds for a project:

  ```ts
  await fetch(`${baseUrl}/web/factory/projects/${projectId}/environment`, {
    method: 'PATCH',
    headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({
      buildTriggers: {
        schedule: { enabled: true, cron: '0 3 * * *', timezone: 'UTC' },
        push: { enabled: true, debounceMinutes: 10 },
      },
    }),
  });
  ```

- Sessions boot every repository in the Factory environment instead of one linked repository. (#26169)

  - The sandbox template is built from every environment repository, in the order shown on the Environment page. Each is cloned under the workspace root, which becomes the session's working directory.
  - The session branch is resumed where the remote has it. A new session branch starts from the latest remote default branch, and local work in an existing checkout is kept.
  - A repository's setup command is skipped when the template already ran it, and the workspace setup command runs last.
  - A `factory-environment` state signal tells the agent which repositories are present, where, and on which branch.
  - Every repository is torn down when the session retires.
  - The agent loads each environment repository's `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` and project skills, starting with the session's own repository. Review sessions load only the session's own repository's instructions, from its base branch.
  - The provider receives the project's stored sandbox settings when a session's sandbox is constructed. Settings saved later apply to sandboxes constructed after that.

  ```ts
  class MyFactorySandbox extends FactorySandbox<{ cpuCount?: number }> {
    readonly provider = 'my-provider';
    readonly settings = z.object({ cpuCount: z.number().int().min(1).optional() });

    create(ctx: FactorySandboxContext, settings: { cpuCount?: number }) {
      // ctx.repos lists every environment repository; ctx.workingDirectory is the workspace root.
      return new MySandbox({ id: ctx.sessionId, cpus: settings.cpuCount ?? 2, workingDirectory: ctx.workingDirectory });
    }
  }
  ```

- Added `FactorySandbox` support to `MastraFactoryConfig.sandbox`. Pass a provider instance (`PlatformFactorySandbox`, `E2BFactorySandbox`, `DockerFactorySandbox`, or the new `LocalFactorySandbox` exported from `@mastra/factory`) and the factory gains that provider's environment settings and template builds. The callback form still works and is wrapped as a `provider: 'custom'` sandbox with no settings. (#26467)

  ```ts
  import { MastraFactory, LocalFactorySandbox } from '@mastra/factory';
  import { E2BFactorySandbox } from '@mastra/e2b';

  // Before: a callback building one sandbox per session
  new MastraFactory({ sandbox: ctx => new E2BSandbox({ id: ctx.sessionId, sandboxId: ctx.sandboxId }) });

  // After: a provider instance, which also carries settings and builds
  new MastraFactory({ sandbox: new E2BFactorySandbox() });

  // Or run sessions on the host machine, one directory per session
  new MastraFactory({ sandbox: new LocalFactorySandbox({ root: '/var/sandboxes' }) });
  ```

- Added a sandbox environment to each Factory project. Provider settings, the workspace setup command, the working directory and the order repositories are checked out in now live on the project and are read and written through one route, `GET` and `PATCH /web/factory/projects/:id/environment`. (#26115)

  ```json
  {
    "settings": { "cpuCount": 8 },
    "workspaceSetupCommand": "pnpm -r build",
    "sandboxWorkingDirectory": "/home/user",
    "repositories": [
      { "projectRepositoryId": "…web", "position": 1 },
      { "projectRepositoryId": "…docs", "position": 2, "inEnvironment": false }
    ]
  }
  ```

  **Settings:** only the keys you set are stored; anything unset keeps the provider default. A patch is merged onto the stored settings, `null` removes a key, and the result is checked against the provider's schema before anything is written. A rejected value answers 400 `invalid_environment` naming the field; a factory without a sandbox answers 400 `no_sandbox`.

  **Repositories:** to reorder, list every repository with positions 1 through n. A second link to the same repository starts outside the environment.

  Existing projects keep working: their links get an order on the next start and nothing is deleted.

- Sessions in a multi-repository environment can push and open change requests in any environment repository. (#26334)

  - Every `source_control_*` tool except `source_control_refresh_change_request_checkout` (the GitLab checkout refresh) takes an optional `repository` slug; the default is the repository the session is filed under, and each tool description lists the valid slugs.
  - The first push to another repository sends the commits checked out there as the session branch, and the agent's later commits in that repository go to the same branch and are sent by its next push.
  - Pushing a repository that has no checkout in the session fails with a clear error.
  - Every push and change request is recorded per repository, and a push after a change request keeps the change request fields.
  - The `gh pr create` observer and auto-subscribe attribute a pull request to its repository and accept any environment repository. Only a pull request from the session branch is recorded on the session; subscribing to someone else's leaves the record alone.
  - `github_subscribe_pr` / `github_unsubscribe_pr` and pull request subscriptions are gated on the Factory, so web user sessions get them too.
  - On the platform GitHub provider, `GH_TOKEN` covers every environment repository when they share one installation and number at most ten; otherwise it covers the session repository and the agent is told to use the `source_control_*` tools for the others.

  For example, an agent in a session filed under `acme/api` pushes and opens a change request in `acme/docs`:

  ```json
  { "tool": "source_control_push_branch", "input": { "repository": "acme/docs" } }
  { "tool": "source_control_create_change_request", "input": { "repository": "acme/docs", "title": "Document the new endpoint" } }
  ```

- **Sessions belong to a Factory.** A session now records the Factory it belongs to, not only the repository link it was created on. This is groundwork for sessions that span every repository in the Factory environment. (#26123)

  - List every session of a Factory the caller can see, across all source-control providers, with `GET /web/factory/projects/:id/sessions`. The sidebar, search and board use it instead of one request per repository.
  - `sessions.create` on the source-control handle now requires the Factory and rejects one that does not match the repository link.

  ```ts
  // Before
  await sourceControl.sessions.create({ sessionId, projectRepositoryId, orgId, userId, branch, baseBranch });

  // After
  await sourceControl.sessions.create({
    sessionId,
    projectRepositoryId,
    factoryProjectId,
    orgId,
    userId,
    branch,
    baseBranch,
  });
  ```

  **Upgrading:** existing sessions are assigned their Factory on the next start. A session whose repository link no longer exists, or that holds the same branch for the same user as an older session on another repository of the Factory, stays unassigned. Unassigned sessions don't appear in the Factory session list, and Slack replies in their threads skip Factory model defaults. Continue that work in a new Slack thread or a new session. A board run that would reuse one fails with an error naming the session to delete before retrying.

#### Patch Changes

- Fixed environment updates that reorder repositories leaving two repositories at the same position. The project fields, settings and every repository change in one `PATCH /web/factory/projects/:id/environment` now save together or not at all, and concurrent reorders apply one after the other. (#26115)

- Fixed concurrent environment settings patches losing updates by merging and validating against the current project inside a database transaction. (#26115)

- Fixed the Factory web UI hanging on the splash screen when authentication is disabled (`auth: null` or `MASTRACODE_AUTH_DISABLED=1`). `GET /auth/me` now returns 404, so the UI shows that no authentication provider is configured instead of retrying forever. (#26797)

- Fixed a reused Factory workspace keeping the reviewer GitHub token when the run-binding lookup fails. A session whose role cannot be read now falls back to the worker token (or repository access), and refresh callbacks from the earlier reviewer context are rejected. The reviewer token comes back once the review binding is readable again. (#26804)

- Redesigned Factory onboarding around an editable draft and a final review. Repository and model choices stay editable and survive redirects and reloads in the onboarding draft. They are applied to the Factory only when you select **Create factory**. If confirmation fails, retrying reuses the same pending Factory instead of creating a duplicate and only replaces repository links onboarding created. **Create factory** stops with a clear message when the chosen model is no longer available. (#26509)

  **Model providers**

  - API keys and provider sign-in are now separate choices, each with explicit organization or personal scope.
  - Provider and model lists offer **Retry** when they fail to load.
  - Members without shared organization access choose a personal model, which becomes both the Factory default and their own default.

  **Codebase and work**

  - Selecting a repository no longer moves you on by itself; **Continue** does.
  - Repository lists and GitLab status offer **Retry** when they fail to load, instead of reporting GitLab as unavailable.
  - Jira, incident.io, and Linear offer **Reconnect** when a connection needs to be authorized again.
  - **Create factory** starts a fresh Factory when the pending one is no longer accessible.

  A contextual illustration follows each step with smooth transitions and continuous work previews. The artwork and animation are implemented in the app, pause when offscreen, and respect reduced-motion settings.

- Fixed enabling `secretEncryption` on an existing Factory database. Model credentials, custom provider API keys, and integration tokens saved before encryption was turned on now read back correctly and are encrypted in place. Previously, they were encrypted as JSON text, so stored credentials stopped working. (#26890)

- Factory skills settings now flag a skill the agent cannot load. Skills are checked with the same frontmatter parser the agent uses, so a `SKILL.md` with invalid YAML (for example an unquoted `: ` in the `description`) shows "Failed to load" with the parse error, instead of looking healthy and then failing at kickoff with "skill not found". (#26788)

- Added a persisted `modelScope` field to Factory projects, defaulting to `factory` to preserve the existing Factory-level behavior. This release saves and exposes the selection for future support; it does not change session model or credential resolution. (#26803)

  Consumers can inspect the saved setting through the exported `FactoryProject` type:

  ```ts
  import type { FactoryProject } from '@mastra/factory';

  function usesFactoryDefaults(project: FactoryProject) {
    return project.modelScope === 'factory';
  }
  ```

- Fixed environment migration to leave the workspace root unset instead of copying a legacy repository checkout path. Preserve explicitly configured roots and cleared values across initialization. (#26115)

- Removed the commit id from the review verdict badge on Reviewing cards. The badge now reads "Approved" or "Changes requested", and still adds "(outdated)" once new commits are pushed after the review. (#26770)

- Sessions with several repositories now report changes and touched files per repository. (#26542)

  - Each repository's changes carry its slug and provider.
  - Touched files are grouped by checkout directory, and files written at the workspace root during the session are listed too. Dotfiles such as `.env` are never listed.
  - The agent's file tools and project path start at the workspace root, and each repository's `AGENTS.md` and project skills load.

  Sessions with one repository work as before.

- Improved GitHub API latency in Factory. GitHub calls for an installation now reuse its access token until it nears expiry, instead of requesting a new token before every call. After the first call, each GitHub call takes about half as long, and Factory requests far fewer tokens, reducing the risk of hitting GitHub's token rate limits. (#26709)

### [@mastra/fastembed@1.3.3](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/fastembed@1.3.3/packages/fastembed/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed embeddings failing with "Config file not found" when the model cache folder is removed, or the home directory changes, while a process is running. The cache folder is now looked up each time a model loads instead of once per process. (#22078)

### [@mastra/langfuse@1.6.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/langfuse@1.6.0/observability/langfuse/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Added top-level observation metadata in Langfuse for scalar span attributes (string, number, or boolean), for example `stepIndex` and `isContinued` on model step spans. Observation-level LLM-as-a-Judge rules can now filter on them. No code change is needed: (#26727)

  ```ts
  import { Observability } from '@mastra/observability';
  import { LangfuseExporter } from '@mastra/langfuse';

  const observability = new Observability({
    configs: {
      langfuse: { exporters: [new LangfuseExporter()] },
    },
  });
  ```

  In Langfuse, filter an observation-level evaluator on `spanType` equals `model_step` and `isContinued` equals `"false"` to judge only the final step of each agent run. Attributes that hold objects or arrays, such as workflow `entryMetadata`, aren't promoted.

### [@mastra/langsmith@1.3.26](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/langsmith@1.3.26/observability/langsmith/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed the LangSmith exporter sending more than one update per run. Span updates now only change the local run, and a single update with the final outputs, metadata and end time is sent when the span ends. Before, LangSmith dropped or rejected the final update, so traces kept partial outputs and a wrong end time. (#26767)

- Fixed LangSmith exporter losing runs: `flush()` now sends queued batches when the client uses `manualFlushMode` (overlapping calls wait for the earlier upload), and `shutdown()` sends the updates for spans it aborts instead of leaving them pending. (#26760)

### [@mastra/libsql@1.26.1](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/libsql@1.26.1/stores/libsql/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed `LibSQLFactoryStorage.withTransaction` on `:memory:` databases. It ran the callback without a transaction, so a write that failed partway left the earlier writes in place. It now commits or rolls back like a file database. (#26115)

- Prevent late workflow result writes from recreating deleted runs. (#26901)

  _Also in: @mastra/pg@1.31.1_

- Fixed observational memory losing context when several operations run at the same time. (#25906)

  - Buffered observations and activated context are no longer lost when reflection, activation, and background buffering overlap, even across processes that share one database file.
  - Fixed `SQLITE_BUSY: database is locked` errors when observational memory and message saves run at the same time on a local file database.
  - With Turso embedded replicas (`url` plus `syncUrl`), an observational memory write syncs the replica before it retries after a conflict, or before it treats a record another instance created as missing. This does not apply to a prebuilt `client`.

  **Upgrading.** A nullable `supersededBy` column is added to the observational memory table automatically at startup. Existing rows are backfilled at the next startup of any process, so older generations stop accepting writes. Protection is complete once every process that shares the database runs the new version; older versions running alongside it neither set nor check the new column.

  If you manage the schema yourself (`disableInit: true` or `MASTRA_DISABLE_STORAGE_INIT=true`), startup does not run this migration. Add a nullable `supersededBy` column, of the same type as `id`, to `mastra_observational_memory` before you upgrade, or reflections and buffered activation fail with a missing-column error.

### [@mastra/loggers@1.3.7](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/loggers@1.3.7/packages/loggers/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed `HttpTransport` and `UpstashTransport` modifying log objects passed to them. When a record has no timestamp, the transport now adds `time` to its own copy, so the caller's object stays unchanged and frozen records are sent instead of failing with a `TypeError`. (#26892)

- Fixed `UpstashTransport` returning empty `{}` log records for list entries that are not valid JSON. Unparseable entries are now skipped, so log queries return only real records and snapshot totals no longer count corrupt entries. (#26891)

### [@mastra/mcp@2.4.0](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/mcp@2.4.0/packages/mcp/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Minor Changes

- Linked MCP calls across traces, in both directions. When an agent or workflow calls a tool through `MCPClient`, the request carries the W3C `traceparent` of the tool call span. `MCPServer` keeps its own trace for the request and links its request span to that span. Its `tools/call` reply names the span that served the call, and `MCPClient` links the tool call span back to it, so you can move between the two traces from either side. When the server uses a tracing bridge and its HTTP instrumentation continues the caller's trace, the request joins that trace instead. (#26059)

  The server reads `traceparent` and `tracestate` from the request `_meta`, or from the HTTP headers that OpenTelemetry-instrumented callers send. It also records them on the request span as `callerTraceparent` and `callerTracestate`, so exporters without span link support still show the caller.

  This needs no setup. To send your own trace context, or none, set `traceContext` on the server:

  ```ts
  const mcp = new MCPClient({
    servers: {
      orders: {
        url: new URL('https://orders.example.com/mcp'),
        traceContext: () => undefined, // send no trace context
      },
    },
  });
  ```

  `@mastra/mcp` now requires `@mastra/core` 1.76.0 or later.

### [@mastra/memory@1.37.1](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/memory@1.37.1/packages/memory/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed observational memory erasing schema-based working memory. This happened when the Observer model returned an empty working-memory object, which Gemini does with `manageWorkingMemory: true`. (#26681)

  An empty working-memory result no longer replaces stored working memory. Mastra now retries the extraction when this happens. Other extracted values, such as the thread title, are kept whether the retry succeeds or fails.

- Fixed "Expression produces a union type that is too complex to represent" type errors in observational memory when the model list grows. Observer, reflector and fallback model lists no longer make type checking scale with the number of model IDs. (#26908)

- Observational Memory no longer blocks an agent step on the Observer as soon as unobserved messages reach `messageTokens` with async buffering enabled. Between `messageTokens` and `observation.blockAfter` (default `1.2`, so 36k tokens with the default 30k threshold), background buffering keeps running and the next ready chunk activates on a later step. A blocking synchronous observation now runs only when pending tokens still reach `blockAfter` after buffered activation, and observes only the uncovered tail. Previously, reaching the threshold without a ready chunk forced a blocking Observer call, and buffering couldn't start above the threshold, so long agent sessions blocked on observation turn after turn. (#22078)

  Unobserved messages can now grow up to `blockAfter` before a step blocks. To keep the previous behavior of observing synchronously at the threshold, set `blockAfter: 1`:

  ```ts
  observationalMemory: {
    observation: { messageTokens: 30_000, blockAfter: 1 },
  }
  ```

  A multiplier `blockAfter` now scales a per-thread `messageTokens` override instead of the instance threshold. With `blockAfter: 1.2`, this thread blocks at 12,000 tokens:

  ```ts
  await memory.updateObservationalMemoryConfig({
    threadId,
    config: { observation: { messageTokens: 10_000 } },
  });
  ```

  `getStatus()` also returns `observationBlockAfter` and `inAsyncObservationBand`.

  At `blockAfter`, activation waits a bounded time for a buffered observation that is still being produced so it activates with the rest. If that takes longer, the step observes the remaining messages synchronously; a late observation whose messages are all already observed is then dropped instead of stored. Activations triggered by the idle TTL or a model change don't wait for buffered observations still in progress.

  Fixed semantic recall missing a turn's new user message when background buffering picked it up on the first step.

- **Fixed out-of-order observations** (#26129)

  Observational memory now keeps observations in the order events happened. Before, an important user message could be listed ahead of earlier events from the same period, so an outdated state looked like the latest one. For example, a "plan not approved, wait for revision instructions" result could be recorded after the user's actual revision instructions, and the agent kept waiting for instructions it already had.

- Fixed `observation.previousObserverTokens` being ignored. Setting a budget now limits Observer calls to the most recent previous observations that fit it, on every observation path, and the Observer is told when its context was truncated. `previousObserverTokens: 0` omits previous observations. The default is now `false`, which sends the full previous observations. That matches how Observer calls have behaved since the budget stopped being applied, so nothing changes unless you set a budget. Stored memory is unaffected: new observations are still appended to the full observation text. (#26193)

- Fixed observational memory buffering end markers going missing from saved message history, and a rare case where an agent's final response content could be overwritten. (#26149)

  When background buffering finished, its end marker was written onto the newest assistant message, usually one the agent was still streaming. The agent's next save of that message dropped the marker. If the agent's last save landed while the marker was being written, the marker write could replace the agent's newer content. The end marker now goes on the message that carries the cycle's start marker, and the message the agent is streaming is never rewritten. The start marker now goes on the newest assistant message the cycle buffers, never on the message the agent continues streaming into, so the end marker can always find it.

- Observational memory now runs its buffering, activation, observation, reflection, and config-override writes for a thread (or resource) one at a time within a process, and a due reflection goes ahead of other waiting writes. Agents and `Memory` instances that share a thread in one process no longer race each other's buffering, activation, and reflection commits, and a reflection is not held back behind a run of buffered-observation writes. Observer and Reflector model calls still run concurrently. If a storage write has not returned after 60 seconds, later writes for that thread stop waiting for it and a warning is logged, so one stuck database call cannot block the thread's memory. (#25998)

- Reflections now keep more of the details users ask about later. The default Reflector instructions tell it to keep every concrete fact (names, places, dates, numbers, amounts, versions, and who said what). They also tell it to keep both the old and new value, with dates, when the user changes their mind, and to keep what the user explicitly said they have never done, do not know, or refused. Reflections favor short factual lines over general summaries, and older observations are condensed in wording rather than stripped of their facts. Custom `reflection.instruction` text is still appended as before. (#26465)

- Fixed observational memory losing context for the agent when background buffering, observation activation, and reflection overlap, including across processes and durable agent steps. (#25906)

  - Observations buffered or activated while a reflection runs are kept in the new generation instead of being dropped and re-observed later in larger batches.
  - Activation and sync observation commit only to the current generation. Messages leave the agent's context only after their observations are saved there; otherwise the observation retries against the current generation or the messages stay in context.
  - A reflection that was superseded by another one no longer marks itself as completed.
  - Messages saved by another agent or process while an observation runs are no longer marked as observed, and an observation that finds nothing new no longer skips messages saved with earlier timestamps.
  - When two agents or processes observe the same messages at once, the second no longer saves a second copy of those observations.
  - Overlapping background buffering calls for the same thread now run one after another, so the same messages are no longer sent to the Observer and stored more than once.
  - An observation or buffered observation that finishes after its thread's observational memory was cleared (or the thread deleted) is discarded instead of being saved into the thread's new record.
  - Copying a thread while a reflection replaces its observational memory no longer gives the copy a record that rejects writes.

### [@mastra/mongodb@1.23.1](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/@mastra/mongodb@1.23.1/stores/mongodb/CHANGELOG.md)
#### Patch Changes

- Fixed observational memory losing buffered observations or activated context when reflections, observation activation, and background buffering overlap, including from several processes. Works on standalone servers and replica sets without transactions. Fixed buffered observations whose text starts with `$` being stored incorrectly. (#25906)

  **Upgrading.** A `supersededBy` field is set on observational memory documents automatically: existing documents are backfilled at the next startup of any process. Protection is complete once every process that shares the database runs the new version. Safe concurrent use relies on the unique `id` index on the observational memory collection, which is created by default; if you use `skipDefaultIndexes`, create it yourself (a warning is logged when it is missing).

- Added run fencing for durable agents. MongoDB stores on replica sets and sharded clusters now reject writes from a durable agent execution that lost its run to crash recovery, so it can't overwrite the recovered run's messages, threads, or workflow state (#23734). (#26423)

  Standalone servers don't support the multi-document transactions this needs, so they keep working as before without run fencing, and durable agents fall back to the PubSub lease. While the server can't be reached to tell which kind it is, durable agent runs fail to start instead of picking a fallback, so two instances can't end up claiming the same run in different ways.

  To prune claims, set `retention.workflows.runOwnership` and `retention.memory.runFences` and call `storage.prune()`.

  Requires `@mastra/core` 1.76.0 or a later 1.x release.

- Fixed `storage.prune()` on MongoDB deleting a document that was updated while the prune was running. A document that is no longer older than `maxAge` when its batch is deleted is now kept. (#26423)

